{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: please review stage two of the adopted lane and worktree identity design at exact head a7360f6c6792f3104b82d9bf39b5b1145ba07423 on request/checkout-creation-and-lane-refs (pushed), one commit over main 8e77fc25. Twenty-two artifacts stand at this head and are the whole diff against main. The reporting artifact is 32902220 at internal/app/checkout_create.go. Governed by request 82327e23 under promise 8af8eaf9, itself the current-protocol successor of the S2 refile; Hugh's adoption ba18cd1a of proposal 7a71886b governs the design.\n\nWhat it delivers: gs worktree creates one checkout for one governing record, resolving the selector through the boundary every other command uses and stamping only the full canonical identifier. It makes a directory, a branch, a private JSON record in the checkout's own Git directory, a commit-message template holding the exact Rests-On line, and one refs/gitseq/lanes/\u003cgoverning event hash\u003e/\u003cattempt\u003e ref. It signs nothing and reads no private key.\n\nEight checks run before anything is written, each reporting one of four words rather than two: refused stops creation on a determinate negative, warned reports a settled governing commitment or a duplicate head behind its own confirmation flag, passed means the check looked and found nothing wrong, and not established means it could not look. That fourth word is the reconciliation the request demanded: an unreadable projection must not be reported as a pass. Four determinate refusals need no projection and still fire.\n\nPlanner's three dispositions are taken: one configured checkout root serving both creation and protection, with unset meaning no boundary rather than a second one; the lane and record readback through the shared captured read and the association's own grader, with the layer-7 contract updated in this same head; and the settled vocabulary shared rather than copied. The hook installer is deliberately absent, because its contract is the clarification going to Hugh; its absence is stated in the command, its reference page, the architecture page and the note.\n\nThree things I am putting to you rather than settling, as planner directed:\n\nFirst, where the shared settled vocabulary belongs. I lifted both word lists into internal/workroom/commitment_status.go beside the status field they describe, and made mergeplan and app read them, because the two copies had opposite defaults. The alternative allowed was a third local copy. Your call.\n\nSecond, the bounded attempt retry. Eight passes, borrowed from the existing claim loop, with contention distinguished from a transient refusal by re-reading the ref. Both the number and that rule are mine, not adopted.\n\nThird, one I raise myself: attempt refs accumulate, are never deleted, and now share the ref inventory's 4,096-ref bound with branches and remote-tracking refs. Exhausting it withholds the whole worktrees response - fail-closed, but a degradation, and a repository with thousands of stamped checkouts would reach it.\n\nTwo things about the filing you should judge rather than take from me. The three documentation pages rest on the behaviour artifacts published in this same set, but their in-file front matter still names pre-existing anchors, because a page cannot cite artifacts whose commit is the commit that contains the page. I did not invent a scheme around that cycle; tell me if you want it solved differently. And the reporting artifact republishes internal/app/checkout_create.go at the same path and commit as its sibling c9559cee, solely so the newest artifact on the promise is the one that reports the stage rather than a design note that happened to land last; the merge retires the sibling.\n\nGates at this head, each return code captured separately and none through a filtering pipe: gofmt empty, go vet rc 0, go build rc 0, git diff --check rc 0, and go test -count=1 ./... rc 0 with 36 packages ok. The implementing agent recorded twenty-one omission controls, each run once with its guard removed, and reported that two needed a second form because the first did not compile - a compile failure is not a red control, and I would rather you re-run the ones you care about than take that count from me. I have not independently re-run the race gate or the documentation gates at this exact head since the rebase; say so if you want them before you sign.\n\nNo schema change, no durable field, no admission rule, no deletion path, and nothing installed in any working repository.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:329022203dfb09dbb65059dec891f85f1a5591d4","branch":"request/checkout-creation-and-lane-refs","conditions":"Independent exact-head review of a7360f6c with explicit Architecture, Security and Simplification conclusions, filed with gs review naming the reporting artifact first.","head":"a7360f6c6792f3104b82d9bf39b5b1145ba07423","no_git_artifact":"true","stale_bases":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:00b18fb3fa8ca966336d9ecd7717ce22d0571be7, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:0f2a72e00b19ccebc5b20d4c88d90bfa40f1817a, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:0fff67d0d742d0914dc99ecb8f03e2613b30354e, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:329022203dfb09dbb65059dec891f85f1a5591d4, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:46da3ea459d849088878ce22dd3789db541ff9aa, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:4810418da27c0bdef01352bab233604ffff7967d, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:4a2684191889218734e902dfcde5177a231e7431, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:4e917adec4baceae81a5d9e3a4cab4e04156c43f, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:52dddf0b5e8230360c3fd53f6046554be2b38cad, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:7013f535fde93b5843f64d4fd73ae2b8bc8bb90d